## sn35.146
sn35.146:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you old deeds, new deeds, the cessation of deeds, and the practice that leads to the cessation of deeds. | T: “Monks, I will teach you new & old kamma, the cessation of kamma, and the path of practice leading to the cessation of kamma.
sn35.146:1.2 | S: Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak. … | T: Listen & pay close attention. I will speak.
sn35.146:1.3 | S: And what is old deeds? | T: “Now what, monks, is old kamma?
sn35.146:1.4 | S: The eye is old deeds. It should be seen as produced by choices and intentions, as something to be felt. | T: The eye is to be seen as old kamma, fabricated & willed, capable of being felt.
sn35.146:1.5 | S: The ear … nose … tongue … body … | T: The ear… The nose…The tongue… The body…
sn35.146:1.6 | S: mind is old deeds. It should be seen as produced by choices and intentions, as something to be felt. | T: The intellect is to be seen as old kamma, fabricated & willed, capable of being felt.
sn35.146:1.7 | S: This is called old deeds. | T: This is called old kamma.
sn35.146:1.8 | S: And what is new deeds? | T: “And what is new kamma?
sn35.146:1.9 | S: The deeds you currently perform by way of body, speech, and mind. | T: Whatever kamma one does now with the body, with speech, or with the intellect:
sn35.146:1.10 | S: These are called new deeds. | T: This is called new kamma.
sn35.146:1.11 | S: And what is the cessation of deeds? | T: “And what is the cessation of kamma?
sn35.146:1.12 | S: When you experience freedom due to the cessation of deeds by body, speech, and mind. | T: Whoever touches the release that comes from the cessation of bodily kamma, verbal kamma, & mental kamma:
sn35.146:1.13 | S: This is called the cessation of deeds. | T: This is called the cessation of kamma.
sn35.146:1.14 | S: And what’s the practice that leads to the cessation of deeds? | T: “And what is the path of practice leading to the cessation of kamma?
sn35.146:1.15 | S: It is simply this noble eightfold path, that is: | T: Just this noble eightfold path:
sn35.146:1.16 | S: right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: right view, right resolve, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration.
sn35.146:1.17 | S: This is called the practice that leads to the cessation of deeds. | T: This is called the path of practice leading to the cessation of kamma.
sn35.146:1.18 | S: So, mendicants, I’ve taught you old deeds, new deeds, the cessation of deeds, and the practice that leads to the cessation of deeds. | T: “So, monks, I have taught you new & old kamma, the cessation of kamma, and the path of practice leading to the cessation of kamma.
sn35.146:1.19 | S: Out of sympathy, I’ve done what a teacher should do who wants what’s best for their disciples. | T: Whatever a teacher should do—seeking the welfare of his disciples, out of sympathy for them—that have I done for you.
sn35.146:1.20 | S: Here are these roots of trees, and here are these empty huts. Practice absorption, mendicants! Don’t be negligent! Don’t regret it later! This is my instruction to you.” | T: Over there are the roots of trees; over there, empty dwellings. Practice jhāna, monks. Don’t be heedless. Don’t later fall into regret. This is our message to you.”
sn35.146:1.21 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.154
sn35.154:1.1 | S: Then a mendicant went up to the Buddha … and asked him, | T: Then a certain monk went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One,
sn35.154:1.2 | S: “Sir, they speak of someone who is ‘accomplished in the faculties’. | T: “‘Consummate in faculties, consummate in faculties,’ it is said.
sn35.154:1.3 | S: How is someone accomplished in the faculties defined?” | T: To what extent is one consummate in faculties?”
sn35.154:2.1 | S: “Mendicant, if someone meditates observing rise and fall in the eye faculty, they grow disillusioned with the eye faculty. | T: “If a monk, while keeping track of arising & passing away with regard to the eye-faculty, becomes disenchanted with the eye-faculty;
sn35.154:2.2 | S: If they meditate observing rise and fall in the ear faculty … nose faculty … tongue faculty … body faculty … | T: if, while keeping track of arising & passing away with regard to the ear-faculty… the nose-faculty… the tongue-faculty… the body faculty…
sn35.154:2.3 | S: mind faculty, they grow disillusioned with the mind faculty. | T: the intellect-faculty, he becomes disenchanted with the intellect-faculty;
sn35.154:2.4 | S: Being disillusioned, desire fades away. … When they’re freed, they know they’re freed. | T: and, disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate; through dispassion, he is released; with release, there is the knowledge, ‘Released’;
sn35.154:2.5 | S: They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’ | T: he discerns that ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for this world,’
sn35.154:2.6 | S: This is how someone who is accomplished in the faculties is defined.” | T: it is to this extent that one is consummate in faculties.”
sn35.154:2.7 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.17
sn35.17:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, if there were no gratification in the eye, sentient beings wouldn’t be aroused by it. | T: “Monks, if there were not this allure to the eye, beings would not become impassioned with the eye.
sn35.17:1.2 | S: But since there is gratification in the eye, sentient beings are aroused by it. | T: But because there is an allure to the eye, beings become impassioned with the eye.
sn35.17:1.3 | S: If the eye had no drawback, sentient beings wouldn’t grow disillusioned with it. | T: “If there were not these drawbacks to the eye, beings would not become disenchanted with the eye.
sn35.17:1.4 | S: But since the eye has a drawback, sentient beings do grow disillusioned with it. | T: But because there are drawbacks to the eye, beings become disenchanted with the eye.
sn35.17:1.5 | S: If there were no escape from the eye, sentient beings wouldn’t escape from it. | T: “If there were not this escape from the eye, beings would not escape from the eye.
sn35.17:1.6 | S: But since there is an escape from the eye, sentient beings do escape from it. | T: But because there is an escape from the eye, beings escape from the eye.
sn35.17:1.7 | S: If there were no gratification in the ear … | T: “If there were not this allure to the ear…
sn35.17:1.8 | S: nose … | T: “If there were not this allure to the nose…
sn35.17:1.9 | S: tongue … | T: “If there were not this allure to the tongue…
sn35.17:1.10 | P: Yasmā ca kho, bhikkhave, atthi jivhāya assādo, tasmā sattā jivhāya sārajjanti. | T: (none)
sn35.17:1.11 | P: No cedaṁ, bhikkhave, jivhāya ādīnavo abhavissa, nayidaṁ sattā jivhāya nibbindeyyuṁ. | T: (none)
sn35.17:1.12 | P: Yasmā ca kho, bhikkhave, atthi jivhāya ādīnavo, tasmā sattā jivhāya nibbindanti. | T: (none)
sn35.17:1.13 | P: No cedaṁ, bhikkhave, jivhāya nissaraṇaṁ abhavissa, nayidaṁ sattā jivhāya nissareyyuṁ. | T: (none)
sn35.17:1.14 | P: Yasmā ca kho, bhikkhave, atthi jivhāya nissaraṇaṁ, tasmā sattā jivhāya nissaranti. | T: (none)
sn35.17:1.15 | S: body … | T: “If there were not this allure to the body…
sn35.17:1.16 | S: mind, sentient beings wouldn’t be aroused by it. | T: “If there were not this allure to the intellect, beings would not become impassioned with the intellect.
sn35.17:1.17 | S: But since there is gratification in the mind, sentient beings are aroused by it. | T: But because there is an allure to the intellect, beings become impassioned with the intellect.
sn35.17:1.18 | S: If the mind had no drawback, sentient beings wouldn’t grow disillusioned with it. | T: “If there were not these drawbacks to the intellect, beings would not become disenchanted with the intellect.
sn35.17:1.19 | S: But since the mind has a drawback, sentient beings do grow disillusioned with it. | T: But because there are drawbacks to the intellect, beings become disenchanted with the intellect.
sn35.17:1.20 | S: If there were no escape from the mind, sentient beings wouldn’t escape from it. | T: “If there were not this escape from the intellect, beings would not escape from the intellect.
sn35.17:1.21 | S: But since there is an escape from the mind, sentient beings do escape from it. | T: But because there is an escape from the intellect, beings escape from the intellect.
sn35.17:2.1 | S: As long as sentient beings don’t truly understand these six interior sense fields’ gratification, drawback, and escape for what they are, they haven’t escaped from this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans—and they don’t live detached, liberated, with mind free of boundaries. | T: “And, monks, as long as beings don’t know as it has come to be—with regard to these six internal sense media—the allure as the allure, the drawbacks as the drawbacks, and the escape as the escape, they have not escaped from this cosmos with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & commonfolk, nor do they dwell disjoined from it, released from it, with unrestricted awareness.
sn35.17:2.2 | S: But when sentient beings truly understand these six interior sense fields’ gratification, drawback, and escape for what they are, they’ve escaped from this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans—and they live detached, liberated, with mind free of boundaries.” | T: “But when beings know as it has come to be—with regard to these six internal sense media—the allure as the allure, the drawbacks as the drawbacks, and the escape as the escape, they have escaped from this cosmos with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & commonfolk, and they dwell disjoined from it, released from it, with unrestricted awareness.”
sn35.17:2.3 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.18
sn35.18:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, if there were no gratification in sights, sentient beings wouldn’t be aroused by them. …” | T: “Monks, if there were not this allure to forms, beings would not become impassioned with forms. But because there is an allure to forms, beings become impassioned with forms. “If there were not these drawbacks to forms, beings would not become disenchanted with forms. But because there are drawbacks to forms, beings become disenchanted with forms. “If there were not this escape from forms, beings would not escape from forms. But because there is an escape from forms, beings escape from forms. “If there were not this allure to sounds… “If there were not this allure to aromas… “If there were not this allure to flavors… “If there were not this allure to tactile sensations… “If there were not this allure to ideas, beings would not become impassioned with ideas. But because there is an allure to ideas, beings become impassioned with ideas. “If there were not these drawbacks to ideas, beings would not become disenchanted with ideas. But because there are drawbacks to ideas, beings become disenchanted with ideas. “If there were not this escape from ideas, beings would not escape from ideas. But because there is an escape from ideas, beings escape from ideas.
sn35.18:1.2 | P: Yasmā ca kho, bhikkhave, atthi rūpānaṁ assādo, tasmā sattā rūpesu sārajjanti. | T: (none)
