## sn35.228
sn35.228:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, an unlearned ordinary person speaks of the ocean. | T: “‘The ocean, the ocean,’ says the uninstructed run-of-the-mill person.
sn35.228:1.2 | S: But that’s not the ocean in the training of the Noble One. | T: But that’s not the ocean in the discipline of the noble ones.
sn35.228:1.3 | S: That’s just a large body of water, a large sea of water. | T: It’s a great mass of water, a great body of water.
sn35.228:1.4 | S: For a person, the eye is an ocean, | T: “The eye is a person’s ocean,
sn35.228:1.5 | S: and its currents are made of sights. | T: and its current consists of forms.
sn35.228:2.1 | S: Someone who can prevail over those currents is said to have crossed over the ocean of the eye, with its waves and whirlpools, its saltwater crocodiles and monsters. | T: Whoever resists that current consisting of forms is said to have crossed over the ocean of the eye with its waves, whirlpools, sharks, & demons.
sn35.228:2.2 | S: Crossed over, gone to the far shore, the brahmin stands on solid ground. | T: Crossed over, gone beyond, one stands on high ground, a brahman.
sn35.228:2.3 | S: For a person, the ear … nose … tongue … body … | T: “The ear is a person’s ocean… “The nose is a person’s ocean… “The tongue is a person’s ocean… “The body is a person’s ocean…
sn35.228:2.4 | P: tassa rasamayo vego. | T: (none)
sn35.228:2.5 | P: Yo taṁ rasamayaṁ vegaṁ sahati, ayaṁ vuccati, bhikkhave, atari jivhāsamuddaṁ saūmiṁ sāvaṭṭaṁ sagāhaṁ sarakkhasaṁ; | T: (none)
sn35.228:2.6 | P: tiṇṇo pāraṅgato thale tiṭṭhati brāhmaṇo …pe… | T: (none)
sn35.228:2.7 | S: mind is an ocean, | T: “The intellect is a person’s ocean,
sn35.228:2.8 | S: and its currents are made of ideas. | T: and its current consists of ideas.
sn35.228:2.9 | S: Someone who can prevail over those currents is said to have crossed over the ocean of the mind, with its waves and whirlpools, its saltwater crocodiles and monsters. | T: Whoever resists that current consisting of ideas is said to have crossed over the ocean of the intellect with its waves, whirlpools, sharks, & demons.
sn35.228:2.10 | S: Crossed over, gone to the far shore, the brahmin stands on solid ground.” | T: Crossed over, gone beyond, one stands on firm ground, a brahman.”
sn35.228:2.11 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: That is what the Blessed One said.
sn35.228:2.12 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: When the One Well-gone had said that, he—the Teacher—said further:
sn35.228:3.1 | S: “A knowledge master who’s crossed <j>the ocean so hard to cross, | T: Whoever crosses over this ocean,
sn35.228:3.2 | S: with its saltwater crocodiles and monsters, <j>its waves, whirlpools, and dangers; | T: with its danger of sharks, demons, waves, so very hard to cross
sn35.228:3.3 | S: they’ve completed the spiritual journey and gone to the end of the world, | T: is called: an attainer of wisdom who has lived the holy life, one who’s attained the end of the cosmos,
sn35.228:3.4 | S: they’re called ‘one who has gone beyond’.” | T: one gone beyond.
sn35.228:3.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.234
sn35.234:1.1 | S: At one time the venerables Ānanda and Udāyī were staying near Kosambī in Ghosita’s monastery. | T: On one occasion Ven. Ānanda and Ven. Udāyin were staying near Kosambī at Ghosita’s monastery.
sn35.234:1.2 | S: Then in the late afternoon, Venerable Udāyī came out of retreat, went to Venerable Ānanda, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Ven. Udāyin, emerging from his seclusion in the evening, went to Ven. Ānanda and exchanged courteous greetings with him.
sn35.234:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to Ānanda: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to Ven. Ānanda,
sn35.234:2.1 | S: “Reverend Ānanda, the Buddha has explained, opened, and revealed in many ways how this body is not-self. | T: “In many ways the body has been pointed out, revealed, and announced by the Blessed One (with these words): ‘For this reason the body is not-self.’
sn35.234:2.2 | S: Is it possible to explain consciousness in the same way? To teach, assert, establish, clarify, analyze, and reveal how consciousness is not-self?” | T: Can consciousness in the same way be declared, taught, described, set forth, revealed, explained, & made plain (with these words): ‘For this reason consciousness is not-self’?”
sn35.234:3.1 | P: “Yatheva kho, āvuso udāyī, ayaṁ kāyo bhagavatā anekapariyāyena akkhāto vivaṭo pakāsito: | T: (none)
sn35.234:3.2 | S: “It is possible, Reverend Udāyī. | T: “It can…
sn35.234:4.1 | S: Does eye consciousness arise dependent on the eye and sights?” | T: Doesn’t eye-consciousness arise in dependence on the eye & forms?”
sn35.234:4.2 | S: “Yes, reverend.” | T: “Yes, friend.”
sn35.234:4.3 | S: “If the cause and reason that gives rise to eye consciousness were to totally and utterly cease without remainder, would eye consciousness still be found?” | T: “And if the cause & reason for the arising of eye-consciousness were to cease totally everywhere, totally in every way without remainder, would eye-consciousness be discerned?”
sn35.234:4.4 | S: “No, reverend.” | T: “No, friend.”
sn35.234:4.5 | S: “In this way, too, it can be understood how consciousness is not-self. | T: “It’s in this way, friend, that consciousness has been pointed out, revealed, and announced by the Blessed One: ‘For this reason consciousness is not-self.’
sn35.234:5.1 | S: Does ear … nose … tongue … body … | T: “Doesn’t ear-consciousness arise in dependence on the ear & sounds?” … “Doesn’t nose-consciousness arise in dependence on the nose & aromas?” … “Doesn’t tongue-consciousness arise in dependence on the tongue & flavors?” … “Doesn’t body-consciousness arise in dependence on the body & tactile sensations?”
sn35.234:5.2 | P: “Evamāvuso”ti. | T: …

