sn35.146:1.14 | S: And what’s the practice that leads to the cessation of deeds? | T: “And what is the path of practice leading to the cessation of kamma?
sn35.146:1.15 | S: It is simply this noble eightfold path, that is: | T: Just this noble eightfold path:
sn35.146:1.16 | S: right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: right view, right resolve, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration.
sn35.146:1.17 | S: This is called the practice that leads to the cessation of deeds. | T: This is called the path of practice leading to the cessation of kamma.
sn35.146:1.18 | S: So, mendicants, I’ve taught you old deeds, new deeds, the cessation of deeds, and the practice that leads to the cessation of deeds. | T: “So, monks, I have taught you new & old kamma, the cessation of kamma, and the path of practice leading to the cessation of kamma.
sn35.146:1.19 | S: Out of sympathy, I’ve done what a teacher should do who wants what’s best for their disciples. | T: Whatever a teacher should do—seeking the welfare of his disciples, out of sympathy for them—that have I done for you.
sn35.146:1.20 | S: Here are these roots of trees, and here are these empty huts. Practice absorption, mendicants! Don’t be negligent! Don’t regret it later! This is my instruction to you.” | T: Over there are the roots of trees; over there, empty dwellings. Practice jhāna, monks. Don’t be heedless. Don’t later fall into regret. This is our message to you.”
sn35.146:1.21 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.154
sn35.154:1.1 | S: Then a mendicant went up to the Buddha … and asked him, | T: Then a certain monk went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One,
sn35.154:1.2 | S: “Sir, they speak of someone who is ‘accomplished in the faculties’. | T: “‘Consummate in faculties, consummate in faculties,’ it is said.
sn35.154:1.3 | S: How is someone accomplished in the faculties defined?” | T: To what extent is one consummate in faculties?”
sn35.154:2.1 | S: “Mendicant, if someone meditates observing rise and fall in the eye faculty, they grow disillusioned with the eye faculty. | T: “If a monk, while keeping track of arising & passing away with regard to the eye-faculty, becomes disenchanted with the eye-faculty;
sn35.154:2.2 | S: If they meditate observing rise and fall in the ear faculty … nose faculty … tongue faculty … body faculty … | T: if, while keeping track of arising & passing away with regard to the ear-faculty… the nose-faculty… the tongue-faculty… the body faculty…
sn35.154:2.3 | S: mind faculty, they grow disillusioned with the mind faculty. | T: the intellect-faculty, he becomes disenchanted with the intellect-faculty;
sn35.154:2.4 | S: Being disillusioned, desire fades away. … When they’re freed, they know they’re freed. | T: and, disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate; through dispassion, he is released; with release, there is the knowledge, ‘Released’;
sn35.154:2.5 | S: They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’ | T: he discerns that ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for this world,’
sn35.154:2.6 | S: This is how someone who is accomplished in the faculties is defined.” | T: it is to this extent that one is consummate in faculties.”
sn35.154:2.7 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

