sn35.234:6.1 | S: mind consciousness arise dependent on the mind and ideas?” | T: “Doesn’t intellect-consciousness arise in dependence on the intellect & ideas?”
sn35.234:6.2 | S: “Yes, reverend.” | T: “Yes, friend.”
sn35.234:6.3 | S: “If the cause and reason that gives rise to mind consciousness were to totally and utterly cease without remainder, would mind consciousness still be found?” | T: “And if the cause & reason for the arising of intellect-consciousness were to cease totally everywhere, totally in every way without remainder, would intellect-consciousness be discerned?”
sn35.234:6.4 | S: “No, reverend.” | T: “No, friend.”
sn35.234:6.5 | S: “In this way, too, it can be understood how consciousness is not-self. | T: “It’s in this way, friend, that consciousness has been pointed out, revealed, and announced by the Blessed One: ‘For this reason consciousness is not-self.’
sn35.234:7.1 | S: Suppose there was a person in need of heartwood. Wandering in search of heartwood, they’d take a sharp axe and enter a forest. There they’d see a big banana trunk, straight and young and grown unskewed. They’d cut it down at the base, cut off the root, cut off the top, and unroll the coiled sheaths. But they wouldn’t even find sapwood, much less heartwood. | T: “It’s just as if a man going around wanting heartwood, seeking heartwood, searching for heartwood, would take a sharp ax and enter a forest. There he would see a large banana tree trunk: straight, young, without shoots. He would cut off the root, cut off the crown, and unfurl the coil of the stem. There he wouldn’t even find softwood, much less heartwood.
sn35.234:7.2 | S: In the same way, a mendicant sees these six fields of contact as neither self nor belonging to self. | T: “In the same way, a monk assumes neither a self nor anything pertaining to a self in the six media of contact.
sn35.234:7.3 | S: So seeing, they don’t grasp anything in the world. | T: Assuming in this way, he doesn’t cling to anything in the world.
sn35.234:7.4 | S: Not grasping, they’re not anxious. Not being anxious, they personally become extinguished. | T: Not clinging, he is not agitated. Unagitated, he is totally unbound right within.
sn35.234:7.5 | S: They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’” | T: He discerns that ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for this world.’”
sn35.234:7.6 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.238
sn35.238:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, suppose there were four venomous vipers of awesome might. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove. Then he addressed the monks, “Monks, suppose there were four vipers of utmost heat & horrible venom.
sn35.238:1.2 | S: Then a person would come along who wants to live and doesn’t want to die, who wants to be happy and recoils from pain. | T: Then a man would come along—desiring life, desiring not to die, desiring happiness, & loathing pain—
sn35.238:1.3 | S: They’d say to him, | T: and people would tell him:
sn35.238:1.4 | S: ‘My man, here are four venomous vipers of awesome might. They must be periodically picked up, washed, fed, and put to sleep. | T: ‘Good man, these four vipers, of utmost heat & horrible venom, are yours. Time after time they must be lifted up, time after time they must be bathed, time after time they must be fed, time after time put to rest.
sn35.238:1.5 | S: But when one or other of these four venomous vipers gets angry with you, you’ll meet with death or deadly pain. | T: And if any of these vipers ever gets provoked with you, then you will meet with death or death-like suffering.
sn35.238:1.6 | S: So then, my man, do what has to be done.’ | T: Do what you think should be done.’
sn35.238:2.1 | S: Then that man, terrified of those four venomous vipers, would flee this way or that. | T: “Then the man—afraid of the four vipers of utmost heat & horrible venom—would flee this way or that.
sn35.238:2.2 | S: They’d say to him, | T: They would tell him,
sn35.238:2.3 | S: ‘My man, there are five deadly enemies chasing you, thinking: “When we catch sight of him, we’ll murder him right there!” | T: ‘Good man, there are five enemy executioners chasing right on your heels, (thinking,) “Wherever we see him, we’ll kill him right on the spot.”
sn35.238:2.4 | S: So then, my man, do what has to be done.’ | T: Do what you think should be done.’
sn35.238:3.1 | S: Then that man, terrified of those four venomous vipers and those five deadly enemies, would flee this way or that. | T: “Then the man—afraid of the four vipers of utmost heat & horrible venom, afraid of the five enemy executioners—would flee this way or that.
sn35.238:3.2 | S: They’d say to him, | T: They would tell him,
sn35.238:3.3 | S: ‘My man, there’s a sixth hidden killer chasing you with a drawn sword, thinking: “When I catch sight of him, I’ll chop off his head right there!” | T: ‘Good man, there is a sixth executioner, a fellow-traveler, chasing right on your heels with upraised sword, (thinking,) “Wherever I see him, I’ll kill him right on the spot.”
sn35.238:3.4 | S: So then, my man, do what has to be done.’ | T: Do what you think should be done.’
sn35.238:4.1 | S: Then that man, terrified of those four venomous vipers and those five deadly enemies and the hidden killer, would flee this way or that. | T: “Then the man—afraid of the four vipers of utmost heat & horrible venom, afraid of the five enemy executioners, afraid of the sixth fellow-traveling executioner with upraised sword—would flee this way or that.
sn35.238:4.2 | S: He’d see an empty village. | T: He would see an empty village.
sn35.238:4.3 | S: But whatever house he enters is vacant, deserted, and empty. | T: Whatever house he entered would be abandoned, void, & empty as he entered it.
sn35.238:4.4 | S: And whatever vessel he touches is vacant, hollow, and empty. | T: Whatever pot he grabbed hold of would be abandoned, void, & empty as he grabbed hold of it.
sn35.238:4.5 | S: They’d say to him, | T: They would tell him,
sn35.238:4.6 | S: ‘My man, there are bandits who raid villages, and they’re striking now. | T: ‘Good man, right now, village-plundering bandits are entering this empty village.
sn35.238:4.7 | S: So then, my man, do what has to be done.’ | T: Do what you think should be done.’
sn35.238:5.1 | S: Then that man, terrified of those four venomous vipers and those five deadly enemies and the hidden killer and the bandits, would flee this way or that. | T: “Then the man—afraid of the four vipers of utmost heat & horrible venom, afraid of the five enemy executioners, afraid of the sixth fellow-traveling executioner with upraised sword, afraid of the village-plundering bandits—would flee this way or that.
sn35.238:5.2 | S: He’d see a large deluge, whose near shore is dubious and perilous, while the far shore is a sanctuary free of peril. | T: He would see a great expanse of water, with the near shore dubious & risky, the further shore secure & free from risk,
sn35.238:5.3 | S: But there’s no ferryboat or bridge for crossing over. | T: but with neither a ferryboat nor a bridge going from this shore to the other.
sn35.238:5.4 | S: Then that man would think, | T: The thought would occur to him,
sn35.238:5.5 | P: ‘ayaṁ kho mahāudakaṇṇavo orimaṁ tīraṁ sāsaṅkaṁ sappaṭibhayaṁ, pārimaṁ tīraṁ khemaṁ appaṭibhayaṁ, natthi ca nāvā santāraṇī uttarasetu vā apārā pāraṁ gamanāya. | T: ‘Here is this great expanse of water, with the near shore dubious & risky, the further shore secure & free from risk, but with neither a ferryboat nor a bridge going from this shore to the other.
sn35.238:5.6 | S: ‘Why don’t I gather grass, sticks, branches, and leaves and make a raft? Riding on the raft, and paddling with my hands and feet, I can safely reach the far shore.’ | T: What if I were to gather grass, twigs, branches, & leaves and, having bound them together to make a raft, were to cross over to safety on the other shore in dependence on the raft, making an effort with my hands & feet?’
sn35.238:6.1 | S: And so that man did exactly that. Having crossed over and gone to the far shore, the brahmin stands on solid ground. | T: Then the man, having gathered grass, twigs, branches, & leaves, having bound them together to make a raft, would cross over to safety on the other shore in dependence on the raft, making an effort with his hands & feet. Crossed over, having gone to the other shore, he would stand on high ground, a brahman.
sn35.238:7.1 | S: I’ve made up this simile to make a point. | T: “Monks, I have made this simile to convey a meaning.
sn35.238:7.2 | S: And this is the point. | T: Here the meaning is this:
sn35.238:7.3 | S: ‘Four venomous vipers of awesome might’ is a term for the four principal states: | T: ‘The four vipers of utmost heat & horrible venom’ stands for the four great elements:
sn35.238:7.4 | S: the elements of earth, water, fire, and air. | T: the earth property, the liquid property, the fire-property, & the wind property.
sn35.238:8.1 | S: ‘Five deadly enemies’ is a term for the five grasping aggregates, that is: | T: ‘The five enemy executioners’ stands for the five clinging-aggregates:
sn35.238:8.2 | S: form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness. | T: the form clinging-aggregate, the feeling clinging-aggregate, the perception clinging-aggregate, the fabrications clinging-aggregate, the consciousness clinging-aggregate.
sn35.238:9.1 | S: ‘The sixth hidden killer with a drawn sword’ is a term for relishing and greed. | T: ‘The sixth fellow-traveling executioner with upraised sword’ stands for passion & delight.
sn35.238:10.1 | S: ‘Empty village’ is a term for the six interior sense fields. | T: “‘The empty village’ stands for the six internal sense media.
sn35.238:10.2 | S: If an astute, competent, intelligent person investigates that in relation to the eye, it appears vacant, hollow, and empty. | T: If a wise, competent, intelligent person examines them from the point of view of the eye, they appear abandoned, void, & empty.
sn35.238:10.3 | S: If an astute, competent, intelligent person investigates that in relation to the ear … nose … tongue … body … | T: If he examines them from the point of view of the ear… the nose… the tongue… the body…
sn35.238:10.4 | S: mind, it appears vacant, hollow, and empty. | T: the intellect, they appear abandoned, void, & empty.
sn35.238:11.1 | S: ‘Bandits who raid villages’ is a term for the six exterior sense fields. | T: ‘The village-plundering bandits’ stands for the six external sense-media.
sn35.238:11.2 | S: The eye is struck by both agreeable and disagreeable sights. | T: The eye is attacked by agreeable & disagreeable forms.
sn35.238:11.3 | S: The ear … | T: The ear is attacked by agreeable & disagreeable sounds.
sn35.238:11.4 | S: nose … | T: The nose is attacked by agreeable & disagreeable aromas.
sn35.238:11.5 | S: tongue … | T: The tongue is attacked by agreeable & disagreeable flavors.
sn35.238:11.6 | S: body … | T: The body is attacked by agreeable & disagreeable tactile sensations.
sn35.238:11.7 | S: mind is struck by both agreeable and disagreeable ideas. | T: The intellect is attacked by agreeable & disagreeable ideas.
sn35.238:12.1 | S: ‘Large deluge’ is a term for the four floods: | T: “‘The great expanse of water’ stands for the fourfold flood:
sn35.238:12.2 | S: the floods of sensual pleasures, desire to be reborn, views, and ignorance. | T: the flood of sensuality, the flood of becoming, the flood of views, & the flood of ignorance.
sn35.238:13.1 | S: ‘The near shore that’s dubious and perilous’ is a term for substantial reality. | T: ‘The near shore, dubious & risky’ stands for self-identification.
sn35.238:14.1 | S: ‘The far shore, a sanctuary free of peril’ is a term for extinguishment. | T: ‘The further shore, secure and free from risk’ stands for unbinding.
sn35.238:15.1 | S: ‘The raft’ is a term for the noble eightfold path, that is: | T: ‘The raft’ stands for just this noble eightfold path:
sn35.238:15.2 | S: right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: right view, right resolve, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration.
sn35.238:16.1 | S: ‘Paddling with hands and feet’ is a term for rousing energy. | T: ‘Making an effort with hands & feet’ stands for the arousing of persistence.
sn35.238:17.1 | S: ‘Crossed over, gone to the far shore, the brahmin stands on solid ground’ is a term for a perfected one.” | T: ‘Crossed over, having gone to the other shore, he would stand on high ground, a brahman’ stands for the arahant.”
sn35.238:17.2 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)


