## sn35.127
sn35.127:1.1 | S: At one time Venerable Bhāradvāja the Alms-Gatherer was staying near Kosambī in Ghosita’s monastery. | T: On one occasion Ven. Piṇḍola Bhāradvāja was staying near Kosambī at Ghosita’s monastery.
sn35.127:1.2 | S: Then King Udena went up to Bhāradvāja the Alms-Gatherer and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then King Udena went to him and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
sn35.127:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: After this exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, the king sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to Ven. Piṇḍola Bhāradvāja:
sn35.127:1.4 | S: “Worthy Bhāradvāja, there are these young monks who are youthful, with pristine black hair, blessed with youth, in the prime of life; and they’ve never played around with sensual pleasures. What is the cause, what is the reason why they practice the full and pure spiritual life as long as they live, maintaining it for a long time?” | T: “What is the reason, Master Bhāradvāja, what is the cause why young monks—black-haired, endowed with the blessings of youth in the first stage of life—without having played with sensuality nevertheless follow the lifelong celibate life, perfect & pure, and make it last their entire lives?”
sn35.127:1.5 | S: “Great king, this has been stated by the Blessed One, who knows and sees, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha: | T: “Great king, this was said by the Blessed One who knows & sees, worthy & rightly self-awakened:
sn35.127:1.6 | S: ‘Please, monks, think of women your mother’s age as your mother. Think of women your sister’s age as your sister. And think of women your daughter’s age as your daughter.’ | T: ‘Come now, monks: with regard to women who are old enough to be your mother, establish the attitude you would have toward your mother. With regard to women who are old enough to be your sister, establish the attitude you’d have toward a sister. With regard to women who are young enough to be your daughter, establish the attitude you’d have toward a daughter.’
sn35.127:1.7 | S: This is a cause, Great King, this is a reason why these young monks practice the full and pure spiritual life as long as they live, maintaining it for a long time.” | T: This is one reason, this is one cause, great king, why young monks—black-haired, endowed with the blessings of youth in the first stage of life—without having played with sensuality nevertheless follow the lifelong celibate life, perfect & pure, and make it last their entire lives.”
sn35.127:2.1 | S: “But worthy Bhāradvāja, the mind is wanton. | T: “The mind is unruly, Master Bhāradvāja.
sn35.127:2.2 | S: Sometimes thoughts of desire come up even for women your mother’s age, your sister’s age, or your daughter’s age. | T: Sometimes thoughts of greed arise even for women who are old enough to be your mother… your sister… young enough to be your daughter.
sn35.127:2.3 | S: Is there another cause, another reason why these young monks live the full and pure spiritual life for their entire life?” | T: Is there another reason, another cause, why young monks… without having played with sensuality nevertheless follow the lifelong celibate life, perfect & pure, and make it last their entire lives?”
sn35.127:3.1 | S: “Great king, this has been stated by the Blessed One, who knows and sees, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha: | T: “Great king, this was said by the Blessed One who knows & sees, worthy & rightly self-awakened:
sn35.127:3.2 | S: ‘Please, monks, examine your own body up from the soles of the feet and down from the tips of the hairs, wrapped in skin and full of many kinds of filth. | T: ‘Come now, monks: reflect on this very body, from the soles of the feet on up, from the crown of the head on down, surrounded by skin, full of all sorts of unclean things:
sn35.127:3.3 | S: In this body there is head hair, body hair, nails, teeth, skin, flesh, sinews, bones, bone marrow, kidneys, heart, liver, diaphragm, spleen, lungs, intestines, mesentery, undigested food, feces, bile, phlegm, pus, blood, sweat, fat, tears, grease, saliva, snot, synovial fluid, urine.’ | T: “In this body there are head hairs, body hairs, nails, teeth, skin, flesh, tendons, bones, bone marrow, kidneys, heart, liver, pleura, spleen, lungs, large intestines, small intestines, gorge, feces, bile, phlegm, pus, blood, sweat, fat, tears, skin-oil, saliva, mucus, fluid in the joints, urine.”’
sn35.127:3.4 | S: This is also a cause, Great King, this is a reason why these young monks live the full and pure spiritual life for their entire life, maintaining it for a long time.” | T: This too is a reason, this too is a cause, great king, why young monks—black-haired, endowed with the blessings of youth in the first stage of life—without having played with sensuality nevertheless follow the lifelong celibate life, perfect & pure, and make it last their entire lives.”
sn35.127:3.5 | S: “This is easy to do for those mendicants who have developed their physical endurance, ethics, mind, and wisdom. | T: “For those who are developed in body, developed in virtue, developed in mind, developed in discernment, Master Bhāradvāja, that isn’t hard to do.
sn35.127:3.6 | S: But it’s hard to do for those mendicants who have not developed their physical endurance, ethics, mind, and wisdom. | T: But for those who are undeveloped in body, undeveloped in virtue, undeveloped in mind, undeveloped in discernment, that is hard to do.
sn35.127:3.7 | S: Sometimes I plan to focus on something as ugly, but only its beauty comes to mind. | T: Sometimes when one thinks, ‘Let’s regard this as unattractive,’ it actually comes to be attractive.
sn35.127:3.8 | S: Is there another cause, another reason why these young monks live the full and pure spiritual life for their entire life?” | T: Is there another reason, another cause, why young monks… without having played with sensuality nevertheless follow the lifelong celibate life, perfect & pure, and make it last their entire lives?”
sn35.127:4.1 | S: “Great king, this has been stated by the Blessed One, who knows and sees, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha: | T: “Great king, this was said by the Blessed One who knows & sees, worthy & rightly self-awakened:
sn35.127:4.2 | S: ‘Please, monks, live with sense doors guarded. | T: ‘Come now, monks: Keep guarding the doors to your sense faculties.
sn35.127:4.3 | S: When you see a sight with your eyes, don’t get caught up in the features and details. | T: On seeing a form with the eye, do not grasp at any theme or variations by which—
sn35.127:4.4 | S: If the faculty of sight were left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure would become overwhelming. For this reason, practice restraint, protect the faculty of sight, and achieve its restraint. | T: if you were to dwell without restraint over the faculty of the eye—evil, unskillful qualities such as greed or distress might assail you. Practice with restraint. Guard the faculty of the eye. Achieve restraint with regard to the faculty of the eye.
sn35.127:4.5 | S: When you hear a sound with your ears … | T: “‘On hearing a sound with the ear…
sn35.127:4.6 | S: When you smell an odor with your nose … | T: “‘On smelling an aroma with the nose…
sn35.127:4.7 | S: When you taste a flavor with your tongue … | T: “‘On tasting a flavor with the tongue…
sn35.127:4.8 | S: When you feel a touch with your body … | T: “‘On feeling a tactile sensation with the body…
sn35.127:4.9 | S: When you know an idea with your mind, don’t get caught up in the features and details. | T: “‘On cognizing an idea with the intellect, do not grasp at any theme or variations by which—
sn35.127:4.10 | S: If the faculty of mind were left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure would become overwhelming. For this reason, practice restraint, protect the faculty of mind, and achieve its restraint.’ | T: if you were to dwell without restraint over the faculty of the intellect—evil, unskillful qualities such as greed or distress might assail you. Practice with restraint. Guard the faculty of the intellect. Achieve restraint with regard to the faculty of the intellect.’
sn35.127:4.11 | S: This is also a cause, Great King, this is a reason why these young monks practice the full and pure spiritual life as long as they live, maintaining it for a long time.” | T: “This too is a reason, this too is a cause, great king, why young monks—black-haired, endowed with the blessings of youth in the first stage of life—without having played with sensuality nevertheless follow the lifelong celibate life, perfect & pure, and make it last their entire lives.”
sn35.127:5.1 | S: “It’s incredible, worthy Bhāradvāja, it’s amazing! | T: “Amazing, Master Bhāradvāja! Astounding!
sn35.127:5.2 | S: How well this was said by the Buddha! | T: How well that has been said by the Blessed One who knows & sees, worthy & rightly self-awakened!
sn35.127:5.3 | S: This is the real cause, this is the reason why these young monks practice the full and pure spiritual life as long as they live, maintaining it for a long time. | T: This is the very reason, this the very cause, why young monks—black-haired, endowed with the blessings of youth in the first stage of life—without having played with sensuality nevertheless follow the lifelong celibate life, perfect & pure, and make it last their entire lives.
sn35.127:5.4 | S: For sometimes I too enter the harem with unprotected body, speech, mind, mindfulness, and sense faculties. At those times powerful thoughts of desire get the better of me. | T: I myself, Master Bhāradvāja: Whenever I enter the inner apartments of the palace unguarded in body, unguarded in speech, unguarded in mind, with mindfulness unestablished and my senses unrestrained, I’m overcome with thoughts of greed.
sn35.127:5.5 | S: But sometimes I enter the harem with protected body, speech, mind, mindfulness, and sense faculties. At those times such thoughts of desire don’t get the better of me. | T: But whenever I enter the inner apartments of the palace guarded in body, guarded in speech, guarded in mind, with mindfulness established and my senses restrained, then I’m not.
sn35.127:5.6 | S: Excellent, worthy Bhāradvāja! Excellent! | T: “Magnificent, Master Bhāradvāja! Magnificent!
sn35.127:5.7 | S: As if he were righting the overturned, or revealing the hidden, or pointing out the path to the lost, or lighting a lamp in the dark so people with clear eyes can see what’s there, the worthy Bhāradvāja has made the teaching clear in many ways. | T: Just as if he were to place upright what was overturned, to reveal what was hidden, to show the way to one who was lost, or to carry a lamp into the dark so that those with eyes could see forms, in the same way has Master Bhāradvāja—through many lines of reasoning—made the Dhamma clear.
sn35.127:5.8 | S: I go for refuge to the Buddha, to the teaching, and to the mendicant Saṅgha. | T: I go to the Blessed One for refuge, to the Dhamma, & to the Saṅgha of monks.
sn35.127:5.9 | S: From this day forth, may the worthy Bhāradvāja remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge for life.” | T: May Master Bhāradvāja remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge from this day forward, for life.”
sn35.127:5.10 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.134
sn35.134:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Sakyans, near the Sakyan town named Devadaha. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying among the Sakyans near a Sakyan town named Devadaha.
sn35.134:1.2 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants: | T: There he addressed the monks,
sn35.134:1.3 | S: “When it comes to the six fields of contact, mendicants, I don’t say that all mendicants have work to do with diligence, nor do I say that none of them have work to do with diligence. | T: “Monks, I don’t say of all monks that they have work to do with heedfulness with regard to the six contact-media. But I don’t say of all monks that they don’t have work to do with heedfulness with regard to the six contact-media.
sn35.134:1.4 | S: I say that, when it comes to the six fields of contact, mendicants don’t have work to do with diligence if they are perfected, with defilements ended, having completed the spiritual journey, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, achieved their heart’s goal, utterly ended the fetter of continued existence, and become rightly freed through enlightenment. | T: “Those monks who are arahants—whose effluents are ended, who have reached fulfillment, done the task, laid down the burden, attained the true goal, laid to waste the fetter of becoming, and who are released through right gnosis: Of them I say that they don’t have work to do with heedfulness with regard to the six contact-media.
sn35.134:1.5 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn35.134:1.6 | S: They’ve done their work with diligence, and are incapable of negligence. | T: They have completed their work with heedfulness. They are incapable of falling back.
sn35.134:1.7 | S: I say that, when it comes to the six fields of contact, mendicants do have work to do with diligence if they are trainees, who haven’t achieved their heart’s desire, but live aspiring to the supreme sanctuary from the yoke. | T: “But as for those monks who are in training, who have not attained the heart’s goal but remain intent on the unsurpassed safety from bondage: Of them I say that they still have work to do with heedfulness with regard to the six contact-media.
sn35.134:1.8 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn35.134:1.9 | S: There are sights known by the eye that are pleasant and also those that are unpleasant. | T: “There are forms cognizable via the eye that are agreeable & disagreeable.
sn35.134:1.10 | S: Though experiencing them again and again they don’t occupy the mind. | T: [When one is heedful] they don’t persist in consuming the mind, even when contacted again & again.
sn35.134:1.11 | S: Their energy is roused up and unflagging, their mindfulness is established and lucid, their body is tranquil and undisturbed, and their mind is immersed and unified in samādhi. | T: When awareness isn’t consumed, persistence is aroused & untiring, the mind concentrated & gathered into singleness.
sn35.134:1.12 | S: Seeing this fruit of diligence, I say that those mendicants have work to do with diligence when it comes to the six fields of contact. … | T: Seeing this fruit of heedfulness, I say of those monks that they still have work to do with heedfulness with regard to the six contact-media.
sn35.134:1.13 | S: There are ideas known by the mind that are pleasant and also those that are unpleasant. | T: “There are sounds cognizable via the ear… aromas cognizable via the nose… flavors cognizable via the tongue… tactile sensations cognizable via the body… “There are ideas cognizable via the intellect that are agreeable & disagreeable.
sn35.134:1.14 | S: Though experiencing them again and again they don’t occupy the mind. | T: [When one is heedful] they don’t persist in consuming the mind, even when contacted again & again.
sn35.134:1.15 | S: Their energy is roused up and unflagging, their mindfulness is established and lucid, their body is tranquil and undisturbed, and their mind is immersed and unified in samādhi. | T: When awareness isn’t consumed, persistence is aroused & untiring, the mind concentrated & gathered into singleness.
sn35.134:1.16 | S: Seeing this fruit of diligence, I say that those mendicants have work to do with diligence when it comes to the six fields of contact.” | T: Seeing this fruit of heedfulness, I say of those monks that they still have work to do with heedfulness with regard to the six contact-media.”
sn35.134:1.17 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.135
sn35.135:1.1 | S: “You’re fortunate, mendicants, so very fortunate, | T: “It’s a gain for you, monks, a great gain,
sn35.135:1.2 | S: to have the opportunity to lead the spiritual life. | T: that you’ve gained the opportunity to live the holy life.
sn35.135:1.3 | S: I’ve seen the hell named ‘Related to the Six Fields of Contact’. | T: I have seen a hell named ‘Six Media of Contact.’
sn35.135:1.4 | S: There, whatever sight you see with your eye is unlikable, not likable; undesirable, not desirable; unpleasant, not pleasant. | T: Whatever form one sees there with the eye is undesirable, never desirable; displeasing, never pleasing; disagreeable, never agreeable.
sn35.135:1.5 | S: Whatever sound you hear … | T: Whatever sound one hears there with the ear…
sn35.135:1.6 | S: Whatever odor you smell … | T: Whatever aroma one smells there with the nose…
sn35.135:1.7 | S: Whatever flavor you taste … | T: Whatever flavor one tastes there with the tongue…
sn35.135:1.8 | S: Whatever touch you feel … | T: Whatever tactile sensation one touches there with the body…
sn35.135:1.9 | S: Whatever idea you know with your mind is unlikable, not likable; undesirable, not desirable; unpleasant, not pleasant. | T: Whatever idea one cognizes there with the intellect is undesirable, never desirable; displeasing, never pleasing; disagreeable, never agreeable.
sn35.135:1.10 | S: You’re fortunate, mendicants, so very fortunate, | T: “It’s a gain for you, monks, a great gain,
sn35.135:1.11 | S: to have the opportunity to lead the spiritual life. | T: that you’ve gained the opportunity to live the holy life.
sn35.135:1.12 | S: I’ve seen the heaven called ‘Related to the Six Fields of Contact’. | T: I have seen a heaven named ‘Six Media of Contact.’
sn35.135:1.13 | S: There, whatever sight you see with your eye is likable, not unlikable; desirable, not undesirable; pleasant, not unpleasant. | T: Whatever form one sees there with the eye is desirable, never undesirable; pleasing, never displeasing; agreeable, never disagreeable.
sn35.135:1.14 | S: Whatever sound … odor … flavor … touch … | T: Whatever sound one hears there with the ear… Whatever aroma one smells there with the nose… Whatever flavor one tastes there with the tongue… Whatever tactile sensation one touches there with the body…
sn35.135:1.15 | S: Whatever idea you know with your mind is likable, not unlikable; desirable, not undesirable; pleasant, not unpleasant. | T: Whatever idea one cognizes there with the intellect is desirable, never undesirable; pleasing, never displeasing; agreeable, never disagreeable.
sn35.135:1.16 | S: You’re fortunate, mendicants, so very fortunate, | T: “It’s a gain for you, monks, a great gain,
sn35.135:1.17 | S: to have the opportunity to lead the spiritual life.” | T: that you’ve gained the opportunity to live the holy life.”
